%I A401841 #8 Nov 12 2026 16:11:46
%S A401841 300,600,1200,2400,2700,4200,4800,5400,7500,9600,10140,10800,14700,
%T A401841 15000,16800,19200,20280,21600,24300,29400,30000,34680,36300,37800,
%U A401841 38400,40560,43200,48600,50700,58800,60000,67200,67500,69360,72600,76800,81120,82500,86400,86700
%N A401841 Numbers m such that there is an integer-sided triangle with area m whose three sides all divide m.
%C A401841 The terms are exactly the numbers j^2*A401840(n) with j >= 1 and n >= 1. Since 300 = A401840(2) is a term, so is j^2*300 for every j >= 1; hence the sequence is infinite. Proof (Start):
%C A401841 Let (X, Y, Z) be an integer-sided triangle of area m such that X, Y, Z all divide m. Put d = gcd(X, Y, Z) and (x, y, z) = (X/d, Y/d, Z/d). The primitive triangle (x, y, z) has rational area K = m/d^2, which is necessarily an integer: for odd perimeter, Heron's formula gives (4*K)^2 congruent to 3 or 7 (mod 8), which is impossible for rational K, whereas for even perimeter K^2 is an integer, forcing rational K to be an integer.
%C A401841 Let L = lcm(x, y, z) and k = L/gcd(L, K). Since x, y, z all divide d*K, we have L | d*K, hence k | d. Writing d = j*k gives m = j^2*k^2*K, where k^2*K is a term of A401840.
%C A401841 Conversely, multiplying all sides of a triangle corresponding to a term of A401840 by any positive integer j gives a triangle whose sides all divide its area j^2*A401840(n). This proves the stated characterization. (End)
%C A401841 All terms are divisible by 60. Proof (Start):
%C A401841 Let x, y, z be the sides of an integer-sided triangle with area m such that x, y, z all divide m. Heron's formula gives (1) (4*m)^2 + (x^2 + y^2 - z^2)^2 = (2*x*y)^2, and the same holds for every permutation of x, y, z.
%C A401841 3 | m and 5 | m: Let t be 3 or 5. In any integer Pythagorean triple A^2 + B^2 = C^2, t divides A*B*C: if t divided none of A, B, C, then A^2, B^2, C^2 would be nonzero squares, which are all 1 modulo 3, and +1 or -1 modulo 5, and A^2 + B^2 = C^2 is impossible in both cases. Suppose t does not divide m. Then t divides none of x, y, z, since they divide m, so t divides neither 4*m nor 2*x*y. Applying the property above to (1), t divides (4*m)*(x^2 + y^2 - z^2)*(2*x*y), hence t | x^2 + y^2 - z^2. The same argument for the other two permutations gives t | x^2 + z^2 - y^2 and t | y^2 + z^2 - x^2. Adding the first two congruences gives t | 2*x^2, so t | x and hence t | m, a contradiction.
%C A401841 4 | m: By Heron's formula, 16*m^2 = (x + y + z)*(-x + y + z)*(x - y + z)*(x + y - z). The four factors have the same parity, and since the product is even they are all even. Put s = (x + y + z)/2, p = s - x, q = s - y, r = s - z (positive integers). Then m^2 = s*p*q*r, s = p + q + r, x = q + r, y = p + r, z = p + q. Let v denote the 2-adic valuation. If p, q, r are all odd, then m is odd, but x = q + r is even and divides m, impossible. If exactly one of them is even, say p, then v(m^2) = v(s) + v(p) >= 2; if v(m) = 1, then v(s) = v(p) = 1, and v(x) <= 1 forces q + r = 2 (mod 4), so s = p + q + r = 0 (mod 4), contradicting v(s) = 1. If exactly two are even, say q and r, then s is odd and 2*v(m) = v(q) + v(r) >= 2; v(m) = 1 would force q = r = 2 (mod 4), hence 4 | x = q + r, contradicting x | m. If all three are even, then v(m^2) >= 4. In every case 4 | m.
%C A401841 Together, 3 | m, 4 | m and 5 | m give 60 | m. (End)
%C A401841 The gcd of all terms is 60, since every term is divisible by 60 and gcd(300, 10140) = 60.
%H A401841 Felix Huber, <a href="/A401841/b401841.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>.
%H A401841 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/HeronianTriangle.html">Heronian Triangle</a>.
%e A401841 300 is a term: The triangle with sides 25, 25, 30 has area 300, and 25, 25, 30 all divide 300.
%e A401841 600 is a term: The triangle with sides 30, 40, 50 (the triangle with sides 3, 4, 5 inflated by 10) has area 600, and 30, 40, 50 all divide 600. This is the smallest term with three different sides.
%e A401841 1200 is a term in two ways: The triangles with sides 50, 50, 60 and 50, 50, 80 both have area 1200, and all their sides divide 1200.
%p A401841 A401841 := proc(n) option remember;
%p A401841     local d, m, q, r, x, y;
%p A401841     m := 0;
%p A401841     if 1 < n then
%p A401841         m := A401841(n - 1);
%p A401841     end if;
%p A401841     do
%p A401841         m := m + 60;
%p A401841         d := NumberTheory:-Divisors(m);
%p A401841         for x in d do
%p A401841             for y in d do
%p A401841                 if x <= y and 2*m <= x*y then
%p A401841                     r := x^2*y^2 - 4*m^2;
%p A401841                     if issqr(r) then
%p A401841                         r := isqrt(r);
%p A401841                         for q in [x^2 + y^2 - 2*r, x^2 + y^2 + 2*r] do
%p A401841                             if issqr(q) and irem(m, isqrt(q)) = 0 then
%p A401841                                 return m;
%p A401841                             end if;
%p A401841                         end do;
%p A401841                     end if;
%p A401841                 end if;
%p A401841             end do;
%p A401841         end do;
%p A401841     end do;
%p A401841 end proc:
%p A401841 seq(A401841(n), n = 1 .. 40);
%Y A401841 Subsequence of A188158.
%Y A401841 Cf. A055595, A083875, A401839, A401840.
